-- The Scramble for Africa scenario City-States have existing Civilopedia entries in the BNW files, but they are much shorter and less detailed
-- For parity with their peers, we edit and expand on those as well
UPDATE Language_en_US
SET Text = 'The Bornu Empire flourished from 1380 to 1893 AD, dominating the lands of what is now northeastern Nigeria, Chad, and parts of Cameroon. It was the successor state to the Kanem Empire, ruled by the Sayfawa dynasty, whose origins stretched back centuries in the central Sahel. The empire''s capital shifted westward over time to better control trade routes and defend against nomadic incursions. Bornu became a hub of Saharan commerce, exporting horses, textiles, and salt, and importing luxury goods from North Africa and beyond.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Under the reign of Idris Alooma (1571-1603), Bornu reached its greatest power, territorial extent, and prosperity. Alooma was a skilled statesman and military strategist who reformed the army, improved administration, and introduced new agricultural practices. His reign brought unprecedented stability, allowing trade to flourish along the trans-Saharan routes. The empire''s influence extended deep into the Sahel, and its scholars and clerics maintained close ties with Islamic centers in Cairo and Mecca.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Though Bornu endured into the late 18th century, it was weakened by internal disputes and external threats. The Fulani Jihad of the early 1800s toppled the kingdom into decades of instability. Conquered by Sudanese forces in 1893, Bornu briefly fell under Islamic Sudanese rule before being absorbed into the British Empire, forming the nucleus of the colony of Nigeria.'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_BORNU_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_zh_CN
SET Text = '博尔努帝国从公元 1380 年到 1893 年繁荣，统治着现在尼日利亚东北部、乍得和喀麦隆部分地区的土地。它是卡内姆帝国的继承国，由赛法瓦王朝统治，其起源可以追溯到几个世纪前的萨赫勒中部地区。随着时间的推移，帝国的首都向西转移，以更好地控制贸易路线并防御游牧民族的入侵。博尔努成为撒哈拉商业的中心，出口马匹、纺织品和盐，并从北非及其他地区进口奢侈品。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]在伊德里斯·阿卢马（Idris Alooma，1571-1603 年）的统治下，博尔努达到了最强大的实力、领土范围和繁荣。阿卢马是一位熟练的政治家和军事战略家，他改革了军队，改善了行政管理，并引入了新的农业实践。他的统治带来了前所未有的稳定，使跨撒哈拉航线沿线的贸易蓬勃发展。帝国的影响力深入萨赫勒地区，其学者和神职人员与开罗和麦加的伊斯兰中心保持着密切的联系。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]尽管博尔努一直延续到 18 世纪末，但它因内部争端和外部威胁而被削弱。 1800 年代初的富拉尼圣战使该王国陷入数十年的不稳定。 1893 年，博尔努被苏丹军队征服，曾短暂受伊斯兰苏丹统治，随后被大英帝国吞并，成为尼日利亚殖民地的核心。'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_BORNU_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_en_US
SET Text = 'In the late 19th century, Msiri, the ambitious son of an East African trader, established the town of Bunkeya in present-day Katanga Province, Democratic Republic of Congo. From this base, he forged the Bayeke Kingdom, controlling a vast territory in the east Congo Basin. Bunkeya became a strategic center of commerce, trading ivory, copper, iron ore, salt, and slaves to Arab-Swahili merchants on the coast. In exchange, Msiri acquired firearms and ammunition, cementing his power and expanding his influence.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]By the 1880s, Bunkeya had drawn the attention of European powers. The British explored potential alliances with Msiri, prompting King Leopold II of Belgium to act swiftly to secure the region for his Congo Free State. Leopold dispatched three expeditions to Bunkeya; the first two failed to secure Msiri''s submission.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]In 1891, a third expedition, led from Zanzibar, forced Bayeke forces to retreat. Msiri was killed during a confrontation shortly thereafter, and Bunkeya was absorbed into the Congo Free State. Its incorporation marked the end of one of the last major indigenous Central African kingdoms to resist European colonial control.'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_BUNKEYA_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_zh_CN
SET Text = '19 世纪末，东非商人雄心勃勃的儿子姆西里 (Msiri) 在今刚果民主共和国加丹加省建立了邦克亚 (Bunkeya) 镇。以此为基地，他建立了巴耶克王国，控制了刚果盆地东部的大片领土。邦克亚成为战略商业中心，向沿海的阿拉伯斯瓦希里商人进行象牙、铜、铁矿石、盐和奴隶的贸易。作为交换，姆西里获得了枪支和弹药，巩固了他的权力并扩大了他的影响力。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]到了 1880 年代，文雅已经引起了欧洲列强的注意。英国探索与姆西里结盟的可能性，促使比利时国王利奥波德二世迅速采取行动，为其刚果自由邦确保该地区的安全。利奥波德派遣了三支探险队前往邦克亚。前两次未能让姆西里屈服。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]1891年，来自桑给巴尔的第三次远征迫使巴耶克军队撤退。不久之后，姆西里在一次冲突中被杀，邦克亚被并入刚果自由邦。它的合并标志着最后一个抵抗欧洲殖民控制的主要中非本土王国之一的终结。'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_BUNKEYA_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_en_US
SET Text = 'The Luba people emerged around the 5th century AD in the Upemba Depression, a resource-rich area of central Africa in modern-day Democratic Republic of Congo. From their early settlements, they cultivated a strong culture centered on fishing, agriculture, and metalworking. By the 16th century, the Luba Kingdom had consolidated under skilled rulers, extending its authority over copper mines, palm oil groves, and fertile lands.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]From the 1580s, successive Luba chieftains expanded their realm rapidly, subduing neighboring peoples and integrating them into a sophisticated system of governance. The Luba became renowned for their artisanship, particularly in woodcarving and metalwork, and for their oral traditions, which preserved the deeds of rulers and the laws of the land. Trade networks brought goods from the Congo River Basin to distant markets.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The arrival of European colonial powers in the late 19th century spelled disaster for the kingdom. Pressured by encroaching Belgian forces, the Luba were formally incorporated into the Congo Free State in 1885. A major rebellion in 1895 sought to restore independence, but Belgian forces crushed the uprising, devastating the Luba population and marking the end of their sovereignty.'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_LUBA_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_zh_CN
SET Text = '卢巴人于公元 5 世纪左右出现在乌彭巴洼地，这是中非资源丰富的地区，位于现代刚果民主共和国。从他们早期的定居点开始，他们就培养了以渔业、农业和金属加工为中心的强大文化。到了 16 世纪，卢巴王国在熟练的统治者的统治下得到巩固，将其权力扩展到铜矿、棕榈油园和肥沃的土地。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]从1580年代起，历代鲁巴土司迅速扩张疆域，征服周边民族，将他们纳入复杂的治理体系。卢巴人因其手工艺（尤其是木雕和金属制品）以及保存统治者行为和当地法律的口头传统而闻名。贸易网络将货物从刚果河流域运送到遥远的市场。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]19世纪末欧洲殖民列强的到来给这个王国带来了灾难。在比利时军队入侵的压力下，卢巴人于 1885 年正式并入刚果自由邦。1895 年发生了一场大规模叛乱，试图恢复独立，但比利时军队镇压了起义，摧毁了卢巴人，标志着他们主权的终结。'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_LUBA_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_en_US
SET Text = 'The Ninsi tribes of the Upper Volta region had long been divided by rivalries and warfare until 1441 AD, when the semi-legendary warrior Wurbi united his people. This victory gave rise to the Mossi Empire, with Wogodogo-later known in French as Ouagadougou-as its capital. Situated at a crossroads of regional trade, Ouagadougou drew merchants from across West Africa, as well as caravans from both the Atlantic and the Sahel.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]For centuries, the Mossi resisted incursions by powerful neighbors, including the Songhai and the Mali Empires, while maintaining trade in gold, salt, and textiles. Ouagadougou grew into a cosmopolitan center of governance, with the emperor presiding over a court that balanced traditional Mossi customs with Islamic influences brought by traders and scholars.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]In 1896, French colonial forces under Paul Voulet and Julien Chanoine swept into the region, seeking to consolidate France''s West African holdings. The Mossi were defeated, and Ouagadougou became part of French West Africa. After independence in 1960, it was named the capital of the Republic of Upper Volta, later renamed Burkina Faso, retaining its role as the political and cultural heart of the nation.'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_OUGADOUGOU_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_zh_CN
SET Text = '上沃尔特地区的宁西部落长期以来因竞争和战争而分裂，直到公元 1441 年，半传奇战士乌尔比 (Wurbi) 团结了他的人民。这次胜利催生了莫西帝国，以沃戈多戈（后来的法语称为​​瓦加杜古）为首都。瓦加杜古位于区域贸易的十字路口，吸引了来自西非各地的商人以及来自大西洋和萨赫勒地区的商队。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]几个世纪以来，莫西家族抵御了包括桑海帝国和马里帝国在内的强大邻国的入侵，同时维持着黄金、盐和纺织品的贸易。瓦加杜古逐渐发展成为一个国际化的治理中心，皇帝主持的法庭平衡了传统的莫西习俗与商人和学者带来的伊斯兰影响。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]1896 年，Paul Voulet 和 Julien Chanoine 领导的法国殖民军队席卷该地区，寻求巩固法国在西非的领土。莫西人被击败，瓦加杜古成为法属西非的一部分。 1960年独立后，它被命名为上沃尔特共和国的首都，后更名为布基纳法索，保留了其作为国家政治和文化中心的地位。'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_OUGADOUGOU_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_en_US
SET Text = 'Founded by the Soninke people around the 11th century AD, Segou rose to prominence as a center of agriculture and trade along the Niger River. Its fertile lands supported farming and fishing, while its markets connected it to other great cities such as Djenne and Timbuktu. The city reached its first golden age under Biton Coulibaly (1712-1755), who transformed it into the capital of the Bambara Kingdom.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Biton''s disciplined army and administrative reforms strengthened Segou''s control over surrounding territories. Under Mamary Coulibaly, Segou joined forces with Djenne and Macina, creating a powerful alliance that dominated the middle Niger region. The city''s reputation for craftsmanship and scholarship grew, with artisans producing fine textiles and metalwork.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Around 1860, the city fell to El Hadj Umar Tall, a Toucouleur conqueror and Islamic reformer, who converted the populace to Islam. French colonial forces arrived in the 1890s, incorporating Segou into their growing West African empire. The French made Segou the administrative center for the Office du Niger irrigation project, cementing its role as a regional hub.'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_SEGOU_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_zh_CN
SET Text = '塞古由索宁克人于公元 11 世纪左右建立，作为尼日尔河沿岸的农业和贸易中心而声名鹊起。其肥沃的土地支持农业和渔业，而其市场则将其与杰内和廷巴克图等其他大城市连接起来。在比顿·库利巴利（Biton Coulibaly，1712-1755 年）的统治下，这座城市达到了第一个黄金时代，他将其转变为班巴拉王国的首都。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]比顿纪律严明的军队和行政改革加强了塞古对周边领土的控制。在马玛丽·库利巴利的领导下，塞古与杰内和马西纳联手，建立了一个统治尼日尔中部地区的强大联盟。这座城市的工艺和学术声誉日益提高，工匠们生产精美的纺织品和金属制品。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]1860 年左右，这座城市被图库勒尔征服者和伊斯兰改革家哈吉·奥马尔·塔尔 (El Hadj Umar Tall) 攻陷，他使民众皈依了伊斯兰教。法国殖民军队于 1890 年代抵达，将塞古并入其不断壮大的西非帝国。法国人将塞古作为尼日尔灌溉项目办公室的行政中心，巩固了其作为区域枢纽的作用。'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_SEGOU_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_en_US
SET Text = 'Perched on steep escarpments at the confluence of two rivers in present-day Nigeria, Sokoto was the fortress - capital of the Sokoto Caliphate, one of the largest Islamic states in 19th-century Africa. Before its rise, the site had been the center of several smaller Islamic kingdoms of pre-colonial Sudan, though their records have been largely lost to history.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The caliphate was established in the early 1800s by Usman dan Fodio following his Fulani-led jihad. Sokoto became the political and spiritual heart of the new state, reaching its peak of influence in the 1820s. It governed a vast network of emirates through a combination of religious authority and military strength, fostering Islamic scholarship and jurisprudence.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The first European to reach Sokoto was the Scottish explorer Hugh Clapperton in 1827. By the turn of the 20th century, British forces sought to control the region as part of their Nigerian colony. In 1903, the English captured Sokoto, dismantling the caliphate and absorbing much of its territory into British Nigeria, ending its two centuries of independence.'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_SOKOTO_TEXT';

UPDATE Language_zh_CN
SET Text = '索科托坐落在现今尼日利亚两条河流交汇处的陡峭悬崖上，是索科托哈里发国的要塞和首都，索科托哈里发国是 19 世纪非洲最大的伊斯兰国家之一。在其崛起之前，该遗址曾是前殖民苏丹的几个较小的伊斯兰王国的中心，尽管它们的记录大部分已消失在历史中。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]1800 年代初，乌斯曼·丹·福迪奥 (Usman dan Fodio) 在富拉尼领导的圣战之后建立了哈里发国。索科托成为新国家的政治和精神中心，其影响力在 1820 年代达到顶峰。它通过宗教权威和军事力量的结合来管理庞大的酋长国网络，促进伊斯兰学术和法学的发展。[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]第一个到达索科托的欧洲人是 1827 年苏格兰探险家休·克拉珀顿 (Hugh Clapperton)。到了 20 世纪之交，英国军队试图控制该地区，将其作为尼日利亚殖民地的一部分。 1903 年，英国占领索科托，瓦解了哈里发国，并将其大部分领土并入英属尼日利亚，结束了其两个世纪的独立。'
WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_SOKOTO_TEXT';
